    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on some year-end stuff for my company and i'm a bit stuck. can you walk me through the straight-line depreciation calculation for a piece of equipment? it cost us $45,000, it's got an estimated salvage value of $5,000, and we expect it to last for 8 years. i just want to make sure i'm doing the steps right for the documentation.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate straight-line depreciation, you first need to find the depreciable base, which is the cost minus the salvage value. So, you subtract the $\\$5,000$ salvage value from the $\\$45,000$ cost. Then, you divide that remaining amount by the useful life of the asset, which is 8 years. This gives you the annual depreciation expense, which is $\\$40,000$ divided by 8, resulting in $\\$5,000$ per year.",
      "source": "target",
