    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. the math is straightforward enough. i guess i'm just wondering about the concept of \"value loss\" itself. from a purely analytical standpoint, why do we treat it as such a predictable, steady decline rather than something that might change based on how much we actually use the machine or how much wear and tear it gets? it feels a bit simplified.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on the core difference between accounting and the real world! The straight-line method is a simplification, not a perfect reflection of reality, which is why it's often called a \"convention.\" It's easier for tax and financial reporting, which is where the rules are set, rather than tracking every single tiny scratch or usage hour. If you wanted a more accurate model reflecting actual usage, you'd look into methods like units-of-production depreciation.",
      "source": "target",
